**Setup.** Place the circle as the unit circle in the complex plane, with point at angle $t$ represented by $e^{it}$. Write the arguments of $A,B,C,D,E,F$ as $a,b,c,d,e,f$.

**Parallelism conditions.** The chord from angle $p$ to angle $q$ has direction $e^{iq}-e^{ip}=2i\sin\!\tfrac{q-p}{2}\,e^{i(p+q)/2}$, hence direction angle $\tfrac{p+q}{2}+\tfrac\pi2\pmod\pi$. Two chords $PQ,RS$ are parallel iff $p+q\equiv r+s\pmod{2\pi}$. Thus
$$a+e=b+d,\qquad b+c=d+f. \tag{1}$$

**The reflection.** The line $CE$ has direction angle $\phi=\tfrac{c+e}{2}+\tfrac\pi2$, so $e^{2i\phi}=-e^{i(c+e)}$. Reflecting $D$ across the line through $C=e^{ic}$ gives
$$X=C+e^{2i\phi}\,\overline{D-C}=e^{ic}-e^{i(c+e)}(e^{-id}-e^{-ic})=e^{ic}+e^{ie}-e^{i(c+e-d)}.$$
Hence
$$X-E=e^{ic}-e^{i(c+e-d)}.$$

**Distance from $B$ to $AC$.** For three points $P(\alpha),Q(\beta),R(\gamma)$ on the unit circle, $\text{dist}(R,\text{line }PQ)=|RQ|\cdot|\sin\angle RQP|=2|\sin\tfrac{\gamma-\beta}{2}|\cdot|\sin\tfrac{\gamma-\alpha}{2}|$ (inscribed angle $=$ half the subtended arc). Therefore
$$\text{dist}(B,AC)=2\Bigl|\sin\tfrac{b-a}{2}\,\sin\tfrac{b-c}{2}\Bigr|. \tag{2}$$

**Distance from $X$ to $EF$.** Using $\text{dist}(z,\text{line }UV)=\dfrac{|\operatorname{Im}((z-U)\overline{(V-U)})|}{|V-U|}$, with $z=X$, $U=E=e^{ie}$, $V=F=e^{if}$:
$$\operatorname{Im}\bigl((X-E)\overline{(F-E)}\bigr)=\operatorname{Im}\bigl((e^{ic}-e^{i(c+e-d)})(e^{-if}-e^{-ie})\bigr).$$
Expanding and using $f=b+c-d$ (from (1)) so that $c-f=d-b$ and $(c+e-d)-f=e-b$ and $(c+e-d)-e=c-d$:
$$=\sin(d-b)-\sin(c-e)-\sin(e-b)+\sin(c-d).$$
Now substitute $e=b+d-a$, i.e. $c-e=c-b-d+a$ and $e-b=d-a$:
$$N:=\sin(d-b)+\sin(c-d)-\sin(c-b-d+a)-\sin(d-a).$$
Group and apply sum-to-product:
$$\sin(d-b)+\sin(c-d)=2\sin\tfrac{c-b}{2}\cos\!\bigl(d-\tfrac{b+c}{2}\bigr),$$
$$\sin(c-b-d+a)+\sin(d-a)=2\sin\tfrac{c-b}{2}\cos\!\bigl(\tfrac{c-b}{2}+a-d\bigr).$$
Thus
$$N=2\sin\tfrac{c-b}{2}\Bigl[\cos\!\bigl(d-\tfrac{b+c}{2}\bigr)-\cos\!\bigl(\tfrac{c-b}{2}+a-d\bigr)\Bigr].$$
With $P=d-\tfrac{b+c}{2}$, $Q=\tfrac{c-b}{2}+a-d$: $\tfrac{P+Q}{2}=\tfrac{a-b}{2}$, $\tfrac{P-Q}{2}=d-\tfrac{a+c}{2}$, so $\cos P-\cos Q=-2\sin\tfrac{a-b}{2}\sin\!\bigl(d-\tfrac{a+c}{2}\bigr)$. Hence
$$N=-4\sin\tfrac{c-b}{2}\,\sin\tfrac{a-b}{2}\,\sin\!\bigl(d-\tfrac{a+c}{2}\bigr).$$

The denominator is $|F-E|=2\bigl|\sin\tfrac{f-e}{2}\bigr|$. From (1), $f-e=(b+c-d)-(b+d-a)=a+c-2d$, so
$$\tfrac{f-e}{2}=\tfrac{a+c}{2}-d=-\bigl(d-\tfrac{a+c}{2}\bigr),\qquad \bigl|\sin\tfrac{f-e}{2}\bigr|=\bigl|\sin\!\bigl(d-\tfrac{a+c}{2}\bigr)\bigr|.$$

Therefore
$$\text{dist}(X,EF)=\frac{|N|}{2|\sin\frac{f-e}{2}|}=\frac{4\bigl|\sin\frac{c-b}{2}\sin\frac{a-b}{2}\sin(d-\frac{a+c}{2})\bigr|}{2\bigl|\sin(d-\frac{a+c}{2})\bigr|}=2\Bigl|\sin\tfrac{b-c}{2}\,\sin\tfrac{b-a}{2}\Bigr|.$$

Comparing with (2):
$$\text{dist}(X,EF)=\text{dist}(B,AC).$$

Thus $X$ is as far from the line $EF$ as $B$ is from $AC$. $\blacksquare$

